# Ollama

Connect hi-shell to [Ollama](https://ollama.ai) for powerful local LLM inference. Ollama provides an easy way to run large language models locally with a simple API.

## Prerequisites

- [Ollama installed](https://ollama.ai) and running
- At least one model pulled (e.g., `ollama pull llama3.2`)

## Setup

### 1. Install and Start Ollama

```bash
# Install Ollama
curl -fsSL https://ollama.ai/install.sh | sh

# Start the server
ollama serve
```

### 2. Pull a Model

```bash
# Recommended models for hi-shell
ollama pull llama3.2          # Good general-purpose model
ollama pull mistral           # Fast and capable
ollama pull codellama         # Optimized for code
ollama pull qwen2.5-coder     # Great for shell commands
```

### 3. Configure hi-shell

```bash
hi-shell --init
# Select "Local" → "Ollama"
# The model name (e.g., "llama3.2")
```

### Manual Configuration

Edit `~/.config/hi-shell/config.toml`:

```toml
llm_provider = "Local"
local_provider = "Ollama"
local_url = "http://localhost:11434"
local_model = "llama3.2"
```

## Configuration Options

| Option | Default | Description |
|--------|---------|-------------|
| `local_url` | `http://localhost:11434` | Ollama API endpoint |
| `local_model` | — | Model name to use |

## Custom Ollama URL

If Ollama is running on a different host or port:

```toml
local_url = "http://192.168.1.100:11434"
```

## Verifying the Connection

Test that hi-shell can connect to Ollama:

```bash
hi-shell list files in current directory
```

If you see a generated command, the connection works.

## Recommended Models

| Model | Size | Quality | Speed | Best For |
|-------|------|---------|-------|----------|
| `llama3.2` | 2.0 GB | Good | Fast | General use |
| `llama3.2:1b` | 1.3 GB | OK | Very fast | Quick commands |
| `mistral` | 4.1 GB | Very good | Medium | Complex commands |
| `codellama` | 3.8 GB | Very good | Medium | Code-focused |
| `qwen2.5-coder` | 4.7 GB | Excellent | Medium | Shell commands |

## Troubleshooting

### "Connection refused" Error

Make sure Ollama is running:

```bash
ollama serve
```

Check that Ollama is listening on port 11434:

```bash
curl http://localhost:11434/api/tags
```

### Model Not Found

Pull the model first:

```bash
ollama pull llama3.2
```

List available models:

```bash
ollama list
```

### Slow Responses

- Use a smaller model (e.g., `llama3.2:1b`)
- Ensure you have enough RAM for the model
- Check if GPU acceleration is working: `ollama ps`
